1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,005
Flatpak is a second way to install software, next to RPM packages.

2
00:00:05,025 --> 00:00:09,980
Each app comes from a Flatpak repository, which Flatpak calls a remote.

3
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:14,035
In this video: configure access to Flatpak repositories.

4
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:21,138
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,608 --> 00:00:24,361
Configure access to Flatpak repositories.

6
00:00:24,831 --> 00:00:29,481
We'll install Flatpak, add a repository, look inside it, turn it off and on,

7
00:00:29,501 --> 00:00:32,000
add one for a single user, and remove it.

8
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:45,255
Flatpak apps do not come from the dnf repositories. They come from Flatpak remotes.

9
00:00:45,275 --> 00:00:47,543
The best known remote is Flathub.

10
00:00:51,013 --> 00:00:55,523
A remote can be system-wide, for every user, or per user, for just you.

11
00:00:55,543 --> 00:00:58,139
Changing the system-wide list needs sudo.

12
00:01:04,659 --> 00:01:09,153
Flatpak is not installed on this machine, so dnf installs it.

13
00:01:09,173 --> 00:01:15,370
That is 108 packages, because Flatpak needs many libraries. The output is shortened.

14
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:26,280
flatpak --version confirms it. Then flatpak remotes lists the repositories.

15
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:31,230
With none added yet, it fails: the repository folder does not exist.

16
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:41,398
flatpak remote-add adds a repository.

17
00:01:41,418 --> 00:01:46,518
First the name we choose, then the address of its dot flatpakrepo file.

18
00:01:46,538 --> 00:01:49,546
We use sudo, so the remote is system-wide.

19
00:01:54,016 --> 00:01:56,919
flatpak remotes now lists flathub, marked system.

20
00:02:00,389 --> 00:02:05,105
Add --show-details for more: the title, the address, and the scope.

21
00:02:13,625 --> 00:02:19,713
flatpak remote-ls lists what a remote offers. --app limits it to applications.

22
00:02:19,733 --> 00:02:23,159
Piped into head -n 5, we see the first five:

23
00:02:23,179 --> 00:02:26,682
the name, the application id, and the branch.

24
00:02:34,202 --> 00:02:39,574
To turn a remote off without deleting it, use remote-modify with --disable.

25
00:02:39,594 --> 00:02:44,678
Now flatpak remotes shows nothing, because disabled remotes are hidden.

26
00:02:49,148 --> 00:02:55,848
--show-disabled brings it back, marked disabled. And --enable turns it on again.

27
00:03:03,368 --> 00:03:07,529
--user adds a remote for your own account only, with no sudo.

28
00:03:07,549 --> 00:03:12,671
Now flatpak remotes shows flathub twice: once for the system, once for you.

29
00:03:19,191 --> 00:03:21,835
flatpak remote-delete removes a remote.

30
00:03:21,855 --> 00:03:26,342
We delete our user one without sudo, and the system one with sudo.

31
00:03:26,362 --> 00:03:27,982
The list is empty again.

32
00:03:33,452 --> 00:03:37,826
We add the system remote back, so the next steps have a repository to use.

33
00:03:45,346 --> 00:03:48,782
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

34
00:03:50,302 --> 00:03:53,385
Remotes are stored on disk, so they survive a reboot.

35
00:03:53,405 --> 00:03:55,786
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

36
00:03:59,256 --> 00:04:02,457
After a fresh boot, flatpak remotes still lists flathub.

37
00:04:09,977 --> 00:04:14,671
Your turn. Add the Flathub repository system-wide, under the name labhub,

38
00:04:14,691 --> 00:04:17,189
with the same address. Then disable it.

39
00:04:20,659 --> 00:04:23,476
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

40
00:04:28,996 --> 00:04:32,496
Next up: Install and remove Flatpak software packages.

41
00:04:32,966 --> 00:04:35,869
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
